    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The seven islands passed from Koli fishing settlements to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Mauryas</a> and later Indian dynasties, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> sultanates, to the Portuguese in 1535 and to the English Crown in 1661, which leased them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> in 1668.</li><li>The Company made Bombay a port and its main base on the west coast, and the Hornby Vellard of 1784, the railway of 1853 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> boom of the 1860s turned the islands into one large trading city.</li><li>Bombay hosted the first session of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> in 1885, became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> in 1960 and took the name Mumbai in the 1990s, after riots and bombings that killed many of its people.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The seven islands of Bombay were joined into a single landmass by the Hornby Vellard, an engineering project begun in 1782 that was first rejected by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> in 1783 and was completed in 1784.</p></aside>
    </section>

