    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">3rd century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The islands become part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">810</span><span class="rf-text">The Silhara dynasty of Konkan begins to rule the region, holding it until 1260</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1535</span><span class="rf-text">The islands are ceded to the Portuguese under the Treaty of Bassein</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1661</span><span class="rf-text">Bombay passes to the English Crown as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-of-braganza">Catherine of Braganza</a>’s dowry</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1668</span><span class="rf-text">The Crown leases the islands to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> for £10 a year</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1784</span><span class="rf-text">The Hornby Vellard is completed and joins the seven islands</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1853</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>’s first passenger railway opens between Bombay and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thane">Thane</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1960</span><span class="rf-text">Bombay becomes the capital of the new state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ashoka"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashoka.jpg" alt="Ashoka" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashoka%27s_visit_to_the_Ramagrama_stupa_Sanchi_Stupa_1_Southern_gateway.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photo Dharma from Sadao, Thailand, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mumbai stands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konkan-coast">Konkan coast</a> of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where a deep natural harbour opens on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a>. The city was built on what was originally an archipelago of seven islands: the Isle of Bombay, Parel, Mazagaon, Mahim, Colaba, Worli and Old Woman’s Island, also called Little Colaba. Pleistocene sediments found near Kandivali in northern Mumbai by the British archaeologist Malcolm Todd in 1939 indicate habitation since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-age">Stone Age</a>, and the Koli fishing community had long lived on the islands. The Koli goddess Mumbadevi, called Mumbā or Mahā-Ambā, is believed to have given the city its name, with ā’ī meaning “mother” in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a>.</p><p>In the 3rd century BCE the islands became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>, and the Satavahanas held them from the 2nd century BCE. The Kanheri Caves became an important centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> in western India. The port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sopara">Sopara</a>, in present-day Nala Sopara, was an important trading centre in the 1st century BCE, with trade contacts with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. In the 2nd century the Greek geographer Ptolemy called the islands Heptanesia, “a cluster of seven islands”. The Portuguese writer Gaspar Correia recorded the name Bombaim after 1512, and in 1516 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duarte-barbosa">Duarte Barbosa</a> used Tana-Maiambu. The derivation of Bombay from a supposed Portuguese phrase for “good little bay” has no scientific basis.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Mumbai&#8221; cites where it covers Etymology, People from Mumbai, Early history:</p><ul><li>M. D. David, History of Bombay, 1661–1708 (University of Mumbai, 1973)</li><li>Henry Yule, A glossary of colloquial Anglo-Indian words and phrases: Hobson-Jobson (Routledge, 1996)</li><li>Thomas Blom Hansen, Wages of violence: naming and identity in postcolonial Bombay (Princeton University Press, 2001)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru.jpg" alt="Jawaharlal Nehru" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nehru_in_the_Netherlands,_1957.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Harry Pot, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under the Portuguese the main trade of the islands was coconuts and coir. The city’s growth began under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">English East India Company</a>, which built a quay, warehouses and a customs house after 1668. Governor Gerald Aungier established the first mint in 1670 and offered business incentives that attracted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parsis">Parsis</a>, Goans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, Dawoodi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohras">Bohras</a>, Gujarati <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banias">Banias</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>. The population rose from about 10,000 in 1661 to 60,000 by 1675. In the late 1680s the Company moved its main holdings from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat">Surat</a> to Bombay. By the end of the 17th century the port traded in salt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, cloth, lead and sword blades with many Indian ports and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>. Shipbuilding began in 1735, and in 1750 Lovji Nusserwanjee Wadia built the Bombay Dock, the first dry dock commissioned in Asia.</p><p>The Hornby Vellard, begun in 1782 and completed in 1784, united the seven islands, and reclamation at Worli and Mahalaxmi followed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>’s first passenger railway ran between Bombay and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thane">Thane</a> from 16 April 1853, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> mill opened at Tardeo in 1854, and the University of Bombay was founded in 1857. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> cut cotton supplies from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and brought a boom in the cotton trade, and the opening of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a> in 1869 changed the port’s sea trade. The Bombay Stock Exchange was established in 1875. Until the 1970s the city’s prosperity rested largely on its textile mills and seaport; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> Port was commissioned at Nhava Sheva in 1989.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Mumbai&#8221; cites where it covers Demographics, Language, Economy:</p><ul><li>R. Swaminathan, Mumbai vision 2015: agenda for urban renewal (Macmillan India in association with Observer Research Foundation, 2006)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bahadur-shah-of-gujarat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bahadur-shah-of-gujarat.jpg" alt="Bahadur Shah of Gujarat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bahadur-shah-of-gujarat">Bahadur Shah of Gujarat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sultan_Bahadur_fleeing_from_the_Fort_of_Cambay_in_1535.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1586-1587 artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Mauryas</a> and Satavahanas the islands were ruled by a succession of dynasties, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-satraps">Western Satraps</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abhira-people">Abhiras</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakataka-dynasty">Vakatakas</a>, Kalachuris, Konkan Mauryas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> and Rashtrakutas. The Shilaharas of Konkan ruled the region from 810 to 1260. In the late 13th century King Bhima, called Bhimdev in one account, set up a kingdom with its capital at Mahikawati, present-day Mahim, and around 1298 the Pathare Prabhus came to Mahim from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Saurashtra</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> took the islands in the late 1340s, and they were then governed by the Gujarat Sultanate. The Gujarat and Bahmani sultanates contested them between 1429 and 1431.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahadur-shah-of-gujarat">Bahadur Shah of Gujarat</a>, apprehensive of the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a>, signed the Treaty of Bassein with the Portuguese on 23 December 1534, and the islands were ceded to them on 25 October 1535. In 1661 the islands passed to the English as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-of-braganza">Catherine of Braganza</a>’s dowry on her marriage to Charles II, and under the Royal Charter of 27 March 1668 the Crown leased them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> for an annual rent of £10. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baji-rao-i">Baji Rao I</a> captured Salsette in 1737 and Bassein in 1739, and Salsette passed to the British in 1774, confirmed by the Treaty of Salbai in 1782. After the Company defeated the last Peshwa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baji-rao-ii">Baji Rao II</a>, in 1817, Bombay was the capital of the Bombay Presidency under British rule. After independence in 1947 it became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombay-state">Bombay State</a>, and from 1 May 1960 of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Mumbai&#8221; cites where it covers Portuguese and British rule, Civic administration, Politics:</p><ul><li>M. D. David, History of Bombay, 1661–1708 (University of Mumbai, 1973)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The city drew many peoples. The Koli were its old fishing community, and the Pathare Prabhus were among its earliest settlers. Under the Portuguese the Roman Catholic orders, particularly the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>, built churches, and the East Indian Catholics were converted by the Portuguese in the 16th century. Dorabji Nanabhoy was the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parsis">Parsi</a> to settle in Bombay, in 1640. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> settled in the 18th century, among them the Bene Israeli from the Konkan villages. In the mid-18th century migrants arrived from many parts of the subcontinent and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> is the most spoken language, followed by Hindi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> and Gujarati, and a colloquial Bombay Hindi is spoken in the streets.</p><p>Bombay was an early centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> and the press: Bhimjee Parikh imported the first printing press in 1670, the Bombay Herald appeared in 1789 and the Bombay Courier in 1790, and the Gujarati newspaper Bombay Samachar has been published since 1822. Elphinstone High School opened in 1822, Wilson College in 1832 and the Grant Medical College in 1845. After the fire of 17 February 1803 destroyed many localities around the Old Fort, the British planned a new town with wider roads. Mumbai is also described as the birthplace of Indian cinema, and the Hindi film industry based there is called Bollywood, a blend of Bombay and Hollywood. Dharavi, in central Mumbai, houses about a million people.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aurangzeb.jpg" alt="Aurangzeb" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aurangzeb-portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cordanrad; derivative work by Rani nurmai, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The islands were attacked often in the 17th century. Yakut Khan, an admiral of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>, landed in October 1672 and ravaged the inhabitants. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> governor-general Rickloffe van Goen attacked on 20 February 1673, and Aungier resisted him. Yakut Khan landed again in 1689, razed Mazagon Fort in June 1690 and left on 8 June 1690 after the British paid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>. A fire on 17 February 1803 razed many localities, and a severe famine in May 1804 led to large-scale emigration. Riots broke out between Muslims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parsis">Parsis</a> in 1851 and again in 1874, and on 11 August 1893 a riot between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and Muslims, after a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> temple was attacked, killed 75 people and injured 350.</p><p>From September 1896 a bubonic plague epidemic struck; the death toll was estimated at 1,900 people a week, and about 850,000 people, half the population, fled. A riot against the plague measures began on 9 March 1898 and led to a strike of dock and railway workers. The Great Bombay Textile Strike began on 18 January 1982, with nearly 250,000 workers in more than 50 mills. Riots in May 1984 killed 278 people and wounded 1,118. After the demolition of the Babri Mosque at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a>, communal riots between Hindus and Muslims in December 1992 and January 1993 killed more than 1,000 people. On 12 March 1993 thirteen coordinated bombs killed 257 people and injured more than 700. Bombs on suburban trains on 11 July 2006 killed 209 and injured over 700. Ten armed men attacked the city from 26 to 29 November 2008, and 164 people died and 308 were injured. Three blasts on 13 July 2011 killed 26.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/elephanta-caves"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elephanta-caves.jpg" alt="Elephanta Caves" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/elephanta-caves">Elephanta Caves</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elephanta_Caves_Trimurti.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Christian Haugen, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Bombay was a base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a> movement. The first session of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> met there from 28 to 31 December 1885, the Rowlatt Satyagraha of 1919 had its main centre in the city, the Quit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> movement was proclaimed there on 7 August 1942, and the Royal Indian Navy Mutiny began on 18 February 1946. The last British troops to leave India, the First Battalion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a> Light Infantry, passed through the arcade of the Gateway of India on 28 February 1948, ending the 282 years of British presence in Bombay. In 1960, after protests by the Samyukta <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> movement in which 105 people were killed by police firing, the Flora Fountain was renamed Hutatma Chowk, “Martyr’s Square”, in their memory.</p><p>The city’s buildings are mostly Gothic Revival, Indo-Saracenic and Art Deco in style. The Victoria Terminus, completed in 1888, was renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhatrapati">Chhatrapati</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a> Terminus in 1996, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elephanta-caves">Elephanta Caves</a>, the terminus and the Victorian and Art Deco Ensemble are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Sites</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> nationalists, led by the Shiv Sena, insisted on the change of the name Bombay to Mumbai in the 1990s, arguing that Bombay echoed colonial rule; some residents still use the old name. Mumbai is the capital of Maharashtra, and more than 12 million people live in the city proper.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/vaishnava-jana-to">Vaishnava Jana To<span>c. 1450</span></a><a href="/songs/sare-jahan-se-accha">Sare Jahan se Accha<span>1904</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Mumbai grew from an archipelago of seven islands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konkan-coast">Konkan coast</a>, held by Indian dynasties, sultanates, the Portuguese and from 1661 the English. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> made it a port and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> city, and it was a base of the independence movement. It is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>.</em></p>

